n\'t know if the rabbit needs the dog. So, this is a conditional that might not be triggered.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think about statement 17: If something is rough, then it visits the lion. And statement 12 says the rabbit is round. Statement 15 says if something is round, then it is rough. So, since the rabbit is round, it is rough. Therefore, the rabbit is rough. Then, from statement 17, since the rabbit is rough, it visits the lion. Which is consistent with statement 14, which says the rabbit visits the lion.\n\nNow, going back to statement 18: If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog. So, if both conditions are true, then the conclusion is true.\n\nBut do we know if the dog needs the rabbit? Let\'s see. The lion needs the dog (statement 8) and the lion needs the rabbit (statement 9). So, the lion needs both, but does the dog need the rabbit? I don\'t see a direct statement. However, statement 19 says if the rabbit visits the lion and the rabbit needs the dog, then the dog is round. The rabbit does visit the lion, so if the rabbit needs the dog, then the dog is round. But we don\'t know if the rabbit needs the dog.\n\nWait, from statement 20, we concluded that the rabbit needs the bald eagle because the bald eagle visits the rabbit. So, the rabbit needs the bald eagle. But does the rabbit need the dog? I don\'t see that stated. So, perhaps the rabbit doesn\'t need the dog, which would mean that statement 19\'s condition isn\'t met, so the dog doesn\'t have to be round. But statement 12 says the rabbit is round, so the dog\'s roundness isn\'t directly affected unless the rabbit needs the dog.\n\nWait, but the rabbit is round, so it\'s rough (statement 15). And since it\'s rough, it visits the lion (statement 17), which is consistent with statement 14.\n\nNow, let\'s think about statement 18 again. If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog. So, if both 