something likes the rabbit, then it chases the lion.\n13. If something needs the lion, then it chases the squirrel.\n14. If the squirrel likes the cow, then the cow does not need the rabbit.\n15. If the squirrel likes the cow and the squirrel is not green, then the squirrel needs the rabbit.\n16. If something needs the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the cow.\n\nWait, but the question is about the rabbit being not rough. Looking at statement 5, it directly says "The rabbit is not rough." So that seems straightforward. But maybe I should check if there are any contradictions or dependencies that could affect this.\n\nLet me list out the roughness:.\n\n- Cow: not rough (statement 1).\n- Lion: no info on roughness, but it\'s green (statement 2).\n- Rabbit: not rough (statement 5).\n- Squirrel: not rough (statement 7).\n\nSo according to the given statements, the rabbit is explicitly stated as not rough. So the statement "The rabbit is not rough" is true.\n\nBut wait, maybe I should check if any other statements could imply something else about the rabbit\'s roughness. Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If the squirrel likes the cow, then the cow does not need the rabbit. But the cow\'s need for the rabbit doesn\'t directly affect the rabbit\'s roughness.\n\nStatement 15: If the squirrel likes the cow and the squirrel is not green, then the squirrel needs the rabbit. But the squirrel\'s color is not mentioned, except that the lion is green. So the squirrel could be green or not, but that doesn\'t affect the rabbit\'s roughness.\n\nStatement 16: If something needs the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the cow. Again, this doesn\'t relate to roughness.\n\nSo, all the other statements don\'t seem to affect the rabbit\'s roughness. The only statement that directly mentions the rabbit\'s roughness is statement 5, which says it\'s not rough</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
