th quiet and smart, so according to this, Harry should be blue.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction. Statement 10 says if something is green and smart, it\'s not blue. But we don\'t have any information that Harry is green, so this might not affect Harry directly.\n\nMoving on, statement 14 says if Harry is smart and blue, then he is red. We\'ve established Harry is smart and, from statement 9, he\'s blue. So, applying statement 14, Harry must be red.\n\nNow, statement 13 states that red things are furry. Since Harry is red, this would mean Harry is furry.\n\nLet me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Harry is smart (7), so he\'s kind (8). Kind things are quiet (11), so Harry is quiet. Quiet and smart things are blue (9), so Harry is blue. Since Harry is smart and blue, he must be red (14). And red things are furry (13), so Harry is furry.\n\nI don\'t see any contradictions in this chain of reasoning. Each step logically follows from the previous one based on the given statements</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
