needs the tiger, from statement 19, the bear must be red. But wait, the bear\'s color isn\'t mentioned directly. However, statement 19 says that if something needs the tiger, it is red. So, the bear needs the tiger, so the bear is red.\n\nNow, the tiger\'s color. Let\'s see if the tiger needs anything that would imply its color. The tiger needs the bald eagle (statement 12) and the rabbit (statement 13). From statement 19, since the tiger needs the bald eagle, does that mean the tiger is red? Wait, no. Statement 19 says if something needs the tiger, then it is red. So, the tiger is the one being needed, not the one needing. So, the tiger\'s need for others doesn\'t directly affect its own color.\n\nWait, but the tiger needs the rabbit. The rabbit is big (statement 8), so from statement 15, the rabbit needs the tiger. Therefore, the rabbit needs the tiger, so from statement 19, the rabbit must be red. So, the rabbit is red.\n\nNow, let\'s look at statement 20: If the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger. Does the bear see the rabbit? The bear sees the bald eagle (statement 6), but does it see the rabbit? The bear does not see the tiger (statement 7), but there\'s no direct statement about the bear seeing the rabbit. So, we don\'t know if the bear sees the rabbit. Therefore, we can\'t conclude anything from statement 20.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the statements about blue. Statement 21 says if something is blue, then it visits the bear. Statement 22 says if something is blue and needs the tiger, then it does not visit the bald eagle.\n\nWe need to find out if the tiger is blue. Let\'s assume the tiger is blue and see if that leads to any contradictions.\n\nIf the tiger is blue, then from statement 21, the tiger visits the bear. But statement 14 says the tiger visits the bear, so that\'s consistent. Now, does the tiger need the tiger? Wait, that doesn\'t make sense. Wait, the tiger needs the bald eagle (statement 12) and the rabbit (statement 13). So, the tiger needs others, but does it need itself? No, that\'s not the case.\n\nWait, statement 22 says if something is blue and needs the tiger, then it does not visit the bald eagle. So, if the tiger is blue, and if the tiger needs the tiger (which it doesn\'t), then it wouldn\'t visit the bald eagle. But since the tiger doesn\'t need itself, this doesn\'t apply. So, maybe the tiger being blue doesn\'t directly conflict with anything.\n\nBut let\'s see if the tiger can be blue. Let\'s check if any statements imply the tiger\'s color.\n\nFrom statement 19, if something needs the tiger, it is red. So, the bear needs the tiger, so the bear is red. The rabbit needs the tiger, so the rabbit is red. The tiger needs the bald eagle and the rabbit, but that doesn\'t affect the tiger\'s color.\n\nIs there any statement that links the tiger\'s color? Let\'s see. The bald eagle is red (statement 2). The bear is red (from needing the tiger). The rabbit is red (from needing the tiger). The tiger\'s color isn\'t directly mentioned.\n\nWait, statement 16: If something needs the tiger and it sees the bald eagle, then the tiger is big. The bald eagle sees the tiger (statement 3), so the tiger is big. So, the tiger is big.\n\nNow, statement 15: If something is big, then it needs the tiger. So, the tiger is big, so it needs the tiger? Wait, that would mean the tiger needs itself, which doesn\'t make sense. Wait, no, statement 15 says if something is big, then it needs the tiger. So, the tiger is big, so it needs the tiger. That seems circular, but perhaps it\'s just a logical structure.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating. Let\'s focus on the tiger\'s color. The only way the tiger could be blue is if there\'s no statement contradicting it. But let\'s see if any statements imply the tiger\'s color.\n\nFrom statement 19, the tiger\'s color isn\'t directly addressed. The only colors mentioned are red and blue. The bald eagle is red, the bear is red, the rabbit is red. The tiger\'s color isn\